        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-16T07:50:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You can get this statically checked.\n\n  private static class Destination\u003cT\u003e {\n    public final State\u003cT\u003e mState; // Or maybe StateInfo\u003cT\u003e ?\n    public final T mEntryData;\n    public Destination(...) {...}\n  }\n\n  private Destination mDestState;\n\n...then in transitionTo(IState\u003cT\u003e state, T entryData) :\n\n  mDestState \u003d new Destination((State\u003cT\u003e) state, entryData);\n\nBecause you can\u0027t instantiate a Destination with disparate types (I mean, without wilful casts at least) you guarantee the compiler checks all accesses, except for this (State\u003cT\u003e) cast that was already there anyway (and is correct).\nIt\u0027s also very useful for you because it will actually let you check the bounds of your generic parameters. I looked at them and I think they are correct, but if we both missed something, this modification here will make the compiler tell you.\n\n\nThis is more changes than it looks because you will have to also update mStateStack, therefore also mTempStateStack – but frankly these should probably be reworked, they are far too complex for what they do (mTempStateStack is utterly useless, it could just be removed in favor of simply following the parentStateInfo link). Chances are you want to do a preliminary refactoring to make this as clean as you need it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 760,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 760,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "809e9add54128a05a0a1b5e7b25dc16bcb56dde4",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
